    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Born into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maryland">Maryland</a>, Douglass taught himself to read, fought back against the &quot;slave-breaker&quot; Edward Covey and escaped north by train in 1838.</li><li>His Narrative of 1845, his speeches and his newspaper the North Star made him a national leader of the abolitionist movement, and he broke with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-lloyd-garrison">William Lloyd Garrison</a> by holding that the Constitution could be used against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>.</li><li>After the Civil War he worked for the rights of freed people and of women, held federal offices in Washington and served as minister to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a>, and he is remembered as the foremost African-American leader of his century.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Douglass was the most photographed American of the 19th century. He never smiled for the camera, so as not to play into the racist caricature of a happy enslaved person.</p></aside>
    </section>

